body:
A method is needed to be able to receive from an existing buffer created by a RenderingDevice.
Just like Texture2Drd:
``` c#

var buffer = new BufferRD();
buffer.Rid = rd.StorageBufferCreate();
ShaderMaterial.SetShaderBuffer("name", buffer );
```

"Ensure support for buffers being shared between shaders" - Will this support compute to fragment binding of arbitrary buffers? I am hoping BufferRD is just for type safety.

In a compute shader I have:
        struct Boid {
	        vec3 position[2];
	        vec3 speed[2];
	        vec4 debug;
        };
        
        layout(set = 0, binding = 0, std430) restrict buffer Flock {
	        Boid boids[];
        } flock;
...
        layout(set = 0, binding = 4, std430) writeonly buffer Output {
	        float m[]; // Transformation matrix and (possibly) additional info.
        } result;

I bind the uniform like this (then update the state in the compute shader):
	var uniforms : Array[RDUniform] = []
...
	populate boid data as a PackedFloat32Array() and throw the bytes downstream
...
	var buffer_rid : RID
	buffer_rid = rendering_device.storage_buffer_create(size, bytes)
	var uniform := RDUniform.new()
	uniform.uniform_type = type
	uniform.binding = binding
	uniform.add_id(buffer_rid)
	uniform_list.append(uniform)

	## And finally, initialize the uniform set ##
	uniform_set = rd.uniform_set_create(uniforms, shader_rid, 0)	
	pipeline = rd.compute_pipeline_create(shader_rid)

I then want to make a new mesh with some debug lines, drawing geometry out from my boids to represent sensors on them (although for tons of compute shaders being able to draw sample structs and draw supporting data would be useful):
        shader_type spatial;
        render_mode unshaded;
        
        struct Boid {
            vec3 position[2];
            vec3 speed[2];
            vec4 debug;
        };
        
        buffer restrict in {
	        Boid boids[50];
        } (bindname = Flock, format = std430);
        
        uniform float vel_scale = 0.2;
        
        varying flat float v_avoidance;
        
        void vertex() {
            int id = int(VERTEX_ID) / 2;
        
            vec3 p = boids[id].position[0];
...

The missing link is basically a material level set_shader_buffer_rd_rid method where I can plugin the RID created when the compute was formed:
	var mat = ShaderMaterial.new()
	mat.shader = debugDrawShaderSpatial
	self.material_override = mat

	mat.set_shader_buffer_rd_rid("Flock", buffer_rid)

Interestingly MultiMesh already has this (kinda) you can mount a uniform into your compute shader from a fragment shader in the native Godot code. It would awesome to mount a compute shader buffer into a spatial buffer GPU->GPU:
	var multimesh_rid := output_mesh_instance.multimesh.get_rid()
	var multimesh_buffer_rid := RenderingServer.multimesh_get_buffer_rd_rid(multimesh_rid)
	assert(multimesh_buffer_rid.is_valid())
	var multimesh_out_uniform := RDUniform.new()
	multimesh_out_uniform.uniform_type = RenderingDevice.UNIFORM_TYPE_STORAGE_BUFFER
	multimesh_out_uniform.binding = 4
	multimesh_out_uniform.add_id(multimesh_buffer_rid)
	uniforms.append(multimesh_out_uniform)

There is a full example of that logic here https://thegodotbarn.com/contributions/snippet/206/basic-flocking-gpu-version
